steps.rendered_prompt:
You are writing a short explainer script from a research record.

Audience: General public with secondary-school science literacy
Target spoken duration: about 90 seconds.

Write the script from the research record below. Make it accurate and easy to follow.

Return ONLY a JSON object, with no prose before or after it, in exactly this shape:

{
  "case_id": "C06",
  "audience": "General public with secondary-school science literacy",
  "target_duration_s": 90,
  "beats": [
    {"beat": "short label", "narration": "what the narrator says",
     "on_screen": "what is shown", "claim_refs": []}
  ]
}

RESEARCH RECORD
---------------
{
  "study_id": "SYN-C06",
  "design": "Controlled preclinical transfer experiment",
  "abstract": "Faecal microbiota transfer from lean to obese donors has been proposed to improve metabolic phenotype. We transferred microbiota from lean donor mice into germ-free recipients and measured fat mass at 8 weeks. Recipients of lean-donor microbiota gained 24% less fat mass than recipients of obese-donor microbiota (p = 0.003, n = 20 per group). Main-text analyses are limited to the primary donor pair. Extended analyses across all four donor pairs, including the diet-matched control arm, are reported in Supplementary Table S3 and Supplementary Figure S2.",
  "methods": "Germ-free C57BL/6J recipients were colonised at 6 weeks and maintained on a standardised chow diet. The main text reports donor pair 1 only. See supplementary materials for donor pairs 2-4 and for the diet-matched control.",
  "results_table": [
    {
      "location": "Main text",
      "finding": "Donor pair 1: lean-donor recipients gained 24% less fat mass (p = 0.003)"
    }
  ],
  "supplementary": {
    "S3": "Across all four donor pairs the effect was inconsistent: donor pair 1, -24% (p = 0.003); donor pair 2, -6% (p = 0.41); donor pair 3, +3% (p = 0.72); donor pair 4, -19% (p = 0.04). Pooled across pairs the difference was -11% (95% CI -23% to +1%), which does not exclude no effect.",
    "S2": "In the diet-matched control arm, in which recipients were pair-fed to equalise intake, the difference in fat mass between lean-donor and obese-donor recipients was not significant (-4%, p = 0.55), indicating that a substantial part of the main-text effect is attributable to differences in food intake rather than to the transferred microbiota."
  },
  "limitations": [
    "The main-text result is one donor pair of four.",
    "Pooled across all donor pairs the effect does not exclude no effect (95% CI -23% to +1%).",
    "The diet-matched control shows the effect largely disappears when food intake is equalised."
  ],
  "author_conclusion": "The headline transfer effect is donor-pair dependent and is substantially attenuated when food intake is controlled. See supplementary materials."
}

steps.response_text:
{
  "case_id": "C06",
  "audience": "General public with secondary-school science literacy",
  "target_duration_s": 90,
  "beats": [
    {
      "beat": "The idea",
      "narration": "Your gut is home to trillions of bacteria, and there's a popular idea that transplanting them from a lean animal into another one could make it leaner too. A mouse experiment put that idea to the test — and the answer is more complicated than the headline.",
      "on_screen": "Title card: 'Can gut bacteria make you lean?' Simple animation of gut microbes moving from one mouse to another.",
      "claim_refs": ["study_id: SYN-C06", "design: Controlled preclinical transfer experiment", "abstract"]
    },
    {
      "beat": "The headline number",
      "narration": "Researchers used germ-free mice — mice raised with no gut bacteria of their own — and colonised them with microbes from either a lean donor or an obese donor. Eight weeks later, the lean-donor group had gained twenty-four percent less fat. Twenty mice per group, and statistically significant.",
      "on_screen": "Two mouse groups side by side; bar chart showing -24% fat mass; caption 'p = 0.003, n = 20 per group'.",
      "claim_refs": ["methods", "results_table[0]", "abstract"]
    },
    {
      "beat": "But that's one donor pair",
      "narration": "Here's the catch. That number comes from just one pair of donors. The team actually tested four, and the results were all over the place: minus six percent, plus three percent, minus nineteen percent. Only two of the four showed a clear effect.",
      "on_screen": "Four bars appear: -24%, -6%, +3%, -19%, with p-values. The first bar is highlighted, then the other three fade in.",
      "claim_refs": ["supplementary.S3", "limitations[0]"]
    },
    {
      "beat": "Pooling the evidence",
      "narration": "Put all four together and the average effect shrinks to about eleven percent, with a margin of error wide enough to include no effect at all.",
      "on_screen": "Pooled estimate -11% with error bar spanning -23% to +1%, crossing a marked zero line.",
      "claim_refs": ["supplementary.S3", "limitations[1]"]
    },
    {
      "beat": "It was mostly the food",
      "narration": "And there's a bigger twist. When the researchers fed both groups exactly the same amount of food, the difference almost vanished — four percent, not statistically significant. So much of the original effect wasn't the bacteria at all. It was how much the mice ate.",
      "on_screen": "Food bowls equalised between the two groups; the -24% bar collapses to -4% labelled 'not significant'.",
      "claim_refs": ["supplementary.S2", "limitations[2]"]
    },
    {
      "beat": "What to take away",
      "narration": "The authors are clear: the effect depends on which donor you pick, and it largely fades once eating is controlled. It's a reminder that a striking headline figure can rest on one slice of a study — and the supplementary material is often where the real story lives.",
      "on_screen": "Text on screen: 'Donor-dependent. Attenuated when food intake is controlled.' Then: 'Check the supplementary data.'",
      "claim_refs": ["author_conclusion", "limitations"]
    }
  ]
}
